@h1 Data: records, collections & access h1@

Wit declares structured data inline with @@@code #name: value code@@@, where the
value is a @strong record strong@ @@@code { … } code@@@, a @strong collection
strong@ @@@code [ … ] code@@@, or a @strong scalar strong@. Declare it once; a
self-organising document reads from it and renders it many ways. Reach into it
from prose with @@@code @name.field code@@@.

@@@pre
#world: { location - Bag End, time - night, storm - true }
pre@@@

For the underlying value system — the six DataValue kinds, JSON mapping,
conditions, and coercion — see @a |href /docs/reference/data-model/| Data model a@.

@h2 Records h2@

A record is a set of @code key/value code@ fields. The separator is a @strong
spaced hyphen strong@ @code  -  code@ or a @strong colon strong@ — both produce
the same field:

@@@pre
#point: { x - 3, y - 7 }
#point: { x: 3, y: 7 }
pre@@@

(fixtures 09-records/inline-single-field, colon-separator.) A nested value omits
the separator entirely — the brace or bracket is the signal:
@@@code { history { years - 31 } } code@@@ and @@@code { tags [ a, b ] } code@@@
(nested-record).

Fields are separated by a @strong comma or a newline strong@, never by bare
whitespace, so inline and block records are identical:

@@@pre
#report: {
  title: Q3 Review
  quarter: 2024
  final: true
}
pre@@@

A trailing comma is tolerated (trailing-comma) and an empty @@@code { } code@@@ is
legal (empty-record). Multi-word keys are stored verbatim —
@code years at post code@ (multi-word-key) — and multi-word values are trimmed
(multi-word-value).

@h2 The comma-in-value trap h2@

An @strong unquoted comma splits fields strong@, because the parser reads it as
a field boundary:

@@@pre
#cite: { author - Boud, D., year - 2024 }   ~ E_MALFORMED_RECORD
pre@@@

That is @code E_MALFORMED_RECORD code@ — "record field missing key" (fixture
09-records/comma-in-value). Quote the value:

@@@pre
#cite: { author: "Boud, D.", year: 2024 }
pre@@@

A quoted string makes its bytes literal (commas, newlines become content) and
forces a string type; only @code \" code@ and @code \\ code@ are recognised
inside, and an unterminated quote is @code E_UNTERMINATED_STRING code@ (fixtures
quoted-string-value, quoted-string-multi-line). A multi-line record value uses
the @code key: code@ / indented-block shape, terminated by a top-level comma or
the closing brace (block-record-multi-line-value and its
-with-close / -with-comma-terminator siblings).

@h2 Collections h2@

A collection is an ordered list in @code [ … ] code@. Items are separated by a
@strong comma or newline — not whitespace strong@. That distinction trips
everyone once:

@@@pre
#a: [ x y z ]     ~ ONE item, the string "x y z"
#b: [ x, y, z ]   ~ THREE items
pre@@@

(fixtures 10-collections/whitespace-separator, inline-values — both verified.)
Multi-word items are kept whole (multi-word-items); per-item typing is mixed
(mixed-types); collections may hold records inline (inline-records) or in blocks
(multi-line-records, multi-line-values) and may nest (nested-collection); a
trailing comma is tolerated (trailing-comma) and an empty @code [ ] code@ is
legal (empty-collection).

@h2 Scalars are typed eagerly h2@

Every bareword value is @strong classified at parse time strong@ into one of four
types, and quoting forces a string. The type is not cosmetic — it drives
conditionals, truthiness, iteration, and how @@@code {{…}} code@@@ coerces a
value. The rules, in order:

@ul
@li @code true code@ or @code false code@ → boolean. li@
@li @code null code@ → null. li@
@li Matches @@@code -?(?:[0-9]+|[0-9]+.[0-9]+) code@@@ → number. li@
@li Anything else → string. li@
ul@

The number pattern is @strong strict strong@: an optional leading minus, then
digits, optionally a single dot and more digits. There is @strong no strong@
leading or trailing bare dot, @strong no strong@ exponent, and @strong no strong@
unary plus. So these all stay @em strings em@: @code .5 code@, @code 5. code@,
@code 1e3 code@, @code +1 code@, and @code "2024" code@ (quoted).

@@@pre
#report: { title - Q3 Review, quarter - 2024, final - true }
pre@@@

@code title code@ is a string, @code quarter code@ a number, @code final code@ a
boolean (fixture 09-records/scalar-types). The same happens per item in a
collection:

@@@pre
#mixed: [ 1, two, true ]
pre@@@

Number, string, boolean respectively (fixture 10-collections/mixed-types).

Classification is not limited to data definitions. Every non-flag parameter
value — through pipes, parens, record-arg, form-fill, or colon-scatter — is run
through the same probe, so a value passed by @em any em@ form can arrive as a
typed number, record, or collection and be a valid @@@code (each) code@@@ target,
@@@code @x.field code@@@ target, or @@@code (if) code@@@ operand.

Conditionals compare by stringifying both sides, so a quoted @code "2024" code@
and a bare @code 2024 code@ @strong do strong@ compare equal — both stringify to
@code 2024 code@. But @code "2024" code@ is not a @em number em@: the distinction
matters wherever a real numeric type is expected, not for equality. See
@a |href /docs/guides/conditionals/| Conditionals a@ for the comparison model.

@h2 Access: fields and indices h2@

Data access reaches into a record or collection from prose:
@@@code @name.field code@@@, @@@code @collection.0.field code@@@, and deeper
chains. It is the @strong verified strong@ way to put data into a sentence — not
@@@code {{…}} code@@@, which does not interpolate in prose. Follow the handle
with a dot and a field name; the chain reads segments until it hits a byte that
is not part of one — a sentence-final period ends it cleanly:

@@@pre
#keeper: { name - Ada }

The keeper was @keeper.name.
pre@@@

Collections index by @strong zero-based number strong@:

@@@pre
#findings: [ { claim - A, strength - high }, { claim - B, strength - low } ]

First: @findings.0.claim. Second strength: @findings.1.strength.
pre@@@

(fixtures 11-data-access/field-access, index-access,
access-into-collection-of-records.) Chains go as deep as the data:
@@@code @keeper.history.years code@@@ (access-into-nested-record) and
@@@code @x.y.z.w code@@@ (deep-chain).

@h2 Fuzzy key matching h2@

Keys match by @strong canonical form strong@: lowercase the key, then keep only
@code [a-z0-9] code@ — spaces, hyphens, and underscores drop out, and camelCase
collapses. So a field stored as @code years at post code@ is reachable by any
space-free spelling:

@@@pre
#keeper: { years at post - 31 }

camel @keeper.yearsAtPost, snake @keeper.years_at_post
pre@@@

Both resolve to @code 31 code@ (verified; fixtures fuzzy-match-camel,
fuzzy-match-snake). If two different keys collapse to the same canonical form,
the access is @code E_AMBIGUOUS_RECORD_KEY code@.

@h2 A space ends the segment h2@

@strong A space terminates the access segment. strong@ So a multi-word key
written with spaces does @em not em@ resolve:

@@@pre
#keeper: { years at post - 31 }

He served @keeper.years at post here.
pre@@@

Only @@@code @keeper.years code@@@ is read — it is unresolved, and @code at post
code@ trails as prose (verified; fixture fuzzy-match-spaces). @strong Multi-word
keys are reachable only through the space-free camel or snake forms above. strong@

@h2 Missing keys degrade, not fail h2@

A missing field is @strong not a parse error strong@ — it is resolved at expand
time and rendered as an unresolved marker
(@code <span class="wit-unresolved"> code@), so a typo shows up in the output
rather than breaking the build (fixture missing-field).

@h2 The value-block form h2@

A complex value that spans lines or contains nodes is wrapped as a value-block
def: @@@code #name: code@@@ … @@@code !! code@@@. This is why some data
definitions end in @@@code !! code@@@ — the block form lets the value hold markup
a single line could not.

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li Leaving an unquoted comma in a value (it splits fields). li@
@li Expecting @@@code [ a b c ] code@@@ to be three items — it is one. li@
@li Expecting @code .5 code@, @code 1e3 code@, or @code +1 code@ to be numbers —
the strict pattern leaves them strings. li@
@li Assuming @code "2024" code@ is a number — it is a string that happens to
compare equal to @code 2024 code@. li@
@li Typing spaces into an access, like @@@code @keeper.years at post code@@@ —
use the camel or snake form. li@
@li Expecting a missing field to fail the build — it degrades gracefully at
expand time. li@
@li Reaching for @@@code {{…}} code@@@ to read data in prose — use
@@@code @field code@@@. li@
ul@

